    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d572">

                <pb facs="0541" n="531" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg531"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 P. W./455</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 82</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-07-24">July 24,
                        1941</date>.<lb/>[Received August 20.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my air mail despatch no.
                    43 of June 24,<anchor n="30" xml:id="d572-f1"/> reporting British military plans
                    for cooperation with the Chinese in event of hostilities between Britain and
                    Japan, I have now the honor to report that according to confidential information
                    which has reached me recently, Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek has approved the
                    British plans with the exception that, regarding the proposed mobile guerrilla
                    units, he is unwilling to agree to any arrangement which would put Chinese
                    forces under command of British officers; such officers must function as
                    advisers or instructors.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C. E. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
